 "event_title"} "Flyweight (≤51 kilograms), Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "25 August –  5 September 1960"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/417"} "Palazzo dello Sport, Roma"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/22975"} "33 from 33 countries"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Format"] [:td {:colspan "2"} "Single elimination tournament."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The Romanian, " [:a {:href "/athletes/7904"} "Mircea Dobrescu"] ", was a slight favorite, having won silver in this class in 1956, after making the quarter-finals in 1952. He would go down in the quarters again in Rome, losing to Japan’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/4269"} "Kiyoshi Tanabe"] ". The final came down to the Soviet " [:a {:href "/athletes/8542"} "Sergey Sivko"] ", who defeated Tanabe in the semis, against Hungary’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/3849"} "Gyula Török"] ", and Török won the gold medal in a very narrow decision, 3-2. It would be Török’s only major international title. Sivko would win the 1961 European crown as a bantamweight, while Tanabe turned professional after winning the 1962 Asian Games bantamweight title. In 1967 he defeated the world flyweight champion, Horacio Accavallo, in a non-title fight, but his career was derailed by an eye injury which rendered him blind in the right eye, and he retired shortly after the Accavallo bout, finishing undefeated as a pro, with 21 wins and one draw."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/3849"} "Gyula Török"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/HUN.png"}] "HUN"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "